        ("How does file compression differ from encryption?",
         ["Identical",
          "Compression makes files smaller (still readable to anyone with a tool); encryption makes them unreadable without a key",
          "Compression is encryption",
          "Both add randomness only"], 1,
         "Files",
         "Some archive tools (zip with password, 7z with AES) combine the two. Encryption alone doesn't reduce size."),
        ("Where do user files live by default on Windows?",
         ["C:\\Windows\\System32",
          "C:\\Users\\<username>\\ with subfolders for Documents, Downloads, Pictures, etc.",
          "/home/<username>",
          "C:\\Program Files"], 1,
         "OS",
         "Knowing the user profile path is key for backup, migration, and troubleshooting. Linux/macOS use /home or /Users."),
        ("How do you reveal hidden files in Windows File Explorer?",
         ["Reformat",
          "View tab > 'Hidden items' (or 'Show > Hidden items' in Windows 11)",
          "Edit BIOS",
          "Restart in Safe Mode"], 1,
         "OS",
         "Hidden files include OS internals like AppData. Don't modify what you don't recognize."),
        ("What does the read-only file attribute do?",
         ["Encrypts",
          "Prevents simple modification or deletion of the file from the file manager unless the attribute is removed",
          "Hides the file",
          "Compresses the file"], 1,
         "OS",
         "Read-only is a soft block, not a real security control. Use NTFS permissions for actual access control."),
        ("What does Disk Cleanup do on Windows?",
         ["Defrags the SSD",
          "Removes temporary files, system caches, and old update artifacts to reclaim disk space",
          "Replaces backup",
          "Encrypts files"], 1,
         "OS",
         "Modern Windows has Storage Sense in Settings for similar automated cleanup."),
        ("What does Windows Task Manager primarily show?",
         ["Disk health",
          "Running apps and processes with CPU/memory/disk/network usage; can end tasks and view startup apps",
          "A productivity software suite",
          "BIOS settings"], 1,
         "OS",
         "Open with Ctrl+Shift+Esc. Useful for stopping a frozen app or finding what is using resources."),
        ("Why boot Windows into Safe Mode for troubleshooting?",
         ["For speed",
          "Loads only essential drivers and services so you can remove malware or roll back a bad driver",
          "Replaces Windows",
          "Enables BIOS"], 1,
         "Troubleshooting",
         "Reach Safe Mode through Settings > Recovery, or hold Shift while clicking Restart, then choose Troubleshoot > Advanced options."),
        ("How does restart differ from shutdown on modern Windows?",
         ["Identical",
          "Shutdown uses Fast Startup (saves kernel state to disk); Restart performs a full clean reboot",
          "Restart is dirty",
          "Shutdown reinstalls the OS"], 1,
         "OS",
         "When troubleshooting, prefer Restart over Shutdown to ensure a fresh kernel start."),
        ("How does sleep differ from hibernate?",
         ["Identical",
          "Sleep keeps RAM powered in low power; hibernate saves RAM to disk (hiberfil.sys) and powers off",
          "Sleep is for backups",
          "Hibernate is wireless"], 1,
         "OS",
         "Hibernate uses ~RAM-size on disk. Useful when battery may run out; resumes to your saved state."),
        ("How do you uninstall an app on Windows?",
         ["Delete the .exe",
          "Settings > Apps > Installed apps (or Control Panel > Programs and Features), then choose Uninstall",
          "Edit registry",
          "Reformat"], 1,
         "OS",
         "Some apps come with their own uninstaller. Avoid manual delete; that leaves registry/leftover files."),
        ("Why prefer automatic updates for an OS and apps?",
         ["More control",
          "They keep security fixes current with little user effort, reducing the window of exposure",
          "Always slower",
          "A built-in OS utility"], 1,
         "Security",
         "Automation closes most attacks of opportunity. Schedule restart windows so updates don't disrupt critical work."),
        ("What does a smart card reader do on a managed PC?",
         ["Plays audio",
          "Authenticates the user via a chip card (often PKI-based) plus a PIN, common in government and large enterprises",
          "A web hosting service",
          "A type of software license"], 1,
         "Security",
         "Smart cards combine 'something you have' and 'something you know'. Resistant to phishing because the secret never leaves the card."),
        ("Why update NIC drivers when network issues persist?",
         ["A productivity software suite",
          "Fixes bugs, improves stability, and addresses security or compatibility issues for your network adapter",
          "Forces Wi-Fi off",
          "A type of file format"], 1,
         "Troubleshooting",
         "Get drivers from the device or PC vendor, not random sites. Roll back drivers via Device Manager if a new one breaks things."),
        ("In an Open/Save dialog, what does the file path show?",
         ["Just the filename",
          "The location of the file in the filesystem hierarchy, including drive/folder names",
          "The owner",
          "Just the date"], 1,
         "OS",
         "Save dialogs default to recent or known locations. Pin frequently used folders for quick access."),
        ("Why are tabs useful in modern web browsers?",
         ["Compression",
          "Multiple pages open in one window, with tab groups, pinning, and easy switching",
          "Replaces extensions",
          "Adds DNS"], 1,
         "Web",
         "Too many tabs eat memory. Browsers offer tab sleeping and group/sync features to manage."),
        ("How does an absolute path differ from a relative path?",
         ["Identical",
          "Absolute starts at the root (C:\\, /); relative is interpreted relative to the current working directory",
          "Absolute is shorter",
          "Relative starts at root"], 1,
         "OS",
         "Use absolute in scripts when location matters; relative for portability inside a project."),
        ("Why use TLS for email (e.g., SMTPS, IMAPS) on a modern mail client?",
         ["A type of computer peripheral",
          "Encrypts the connection between client and server, protecting credentials and message content from eavesdropping",
          "Removes spam",
          "Compresses messages"], 1,
         "Email",
         "Modern providers require TLS. Use port 587 + STARTTLS or 465 SMTPS for submission, 993 IMAPS, 995 POP3S."),
        ("Which is a safe practice for handling unexpected email attachments?",
         ["Open immediately",
          "Confirm sender via another channel, scan with antivirus, and don't enable macros unless you trust the source",
          "Run as admin",
          "Forward to all"], 1,
         "Email security",
         "Attachments are a top malware vector. Phishing-resistant policies (block macros from internet, sandboxing) help at the org level."),
